practice.chat.user.template:
The learner level is {{level}}. Continue a friendly {{learning_language_native_name}} practice chat. {{learning_focus_instruction}}{{practice_topic_instruction}}Use the recent learner messages as short context, but answer the latest message. Reply mostly in {{interface_language_native_name}} with one short {{learning_language_native_name}} phrase or question. If there are mistakes, briefly correct only the most useful one in {{interface_language_native_name}}; if a previous mistake or weak phrase appears in the recent context, recycle it naturally once. Keep the learner speaking: model one natural chunk, then ask for one small answer that reuses it. The {{model_phrase_label}} block must contain only one clean {{learning_language_native_name}} sentence for pronunciation. Do not add translations, transliteration, explanations, or parenthesized interface-language text inside that block. Ask only one simple {{learning_language_native_name}} question at the end, and put that question on its own final line. Use clean plain text with visibly separated blocks using exactly these localized labels: {{practice_prompt_labels}}. Use bullets when listing alternatives. Do not use English section labels unless the interface language is English. Do not use design/style words such as saturation, gradient, prompt, or UI. Do not send one dense paragraph. Use 0-2 relevant emojis. Do not use Markdown or asterisks in your reply. {{recent_context}}Latest learner message: {{latest_learner_message}}

After your reply, on a new line write exactly: ---MISTAKES---
Then write a JSON array of grammar or vocabulary mistakes found in the learner's message. Each object: "word" (wrong phrase), "correction" (correct version in {{learning_language_native_name}}), "explanation" (one sentence in {{interface_language_native_name}}). If no mistakes, write [].

